# `qp::WarmStart`

A starting point for a solve: the primal variables, the constraint values and the multipliers of a previous solution.

`WarmStart` holds three `Series` that together describe where the iteration begins: `x` (the variables), `z` (the constraint values `A x`) and `y` (the multipliers). Obtain one from a finished solve with [`Result::warm()`](Result.md); pass it as the last argument of `qp::solve` or of `Solver::solve`. An empty `WarmStart` (the default) means a cold start from zero. A warm start pays off when the new problem is close to the one that produced it: the iteration needs fewer steps to converge. It never changes the answer, only the work.

## Fields

### `x`

`pluss::Series`, default `empty`.

The starting value of the variables.

### `z`

`pluss::Series`, default `empty`.

The starting constraint values (`A x`).

### `y`

`pluss::Series`, default `empty`.

The starting multipliers.

## Guidance

An empty `WarmStart` is a cold start.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

Warm start from a previous solution. After a small change to `q`, restarting from `first.warm()` takes fewer iterations than a cold start.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <limits>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace qp = pluss::subsp::math::qp;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  // minimise x0^2 + x1^2 - 2 x0 - 5 x1 = 0.5 x'Px + q'x   subject to   x0 + x1 = 1,  0 <= x0 <= 0.7,  x1 >= 0
  const double inf = std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity();
  const Series P = pluss::series::make({{2.0, 0.0}, {0.0, 2.0}});
  const Series q = pluss::series::make({-2.0, -5.0});
  const Series A = pluss::series::make({{1.0, 1.0}, {1.0, 0.0}, {0.0, 1.0}});
  const Series lower = pluss::series::make({1.0, 0.0, 0.0});
  const Series upper = pluss::series::make({1.0, 0.7, inf});

  const qp::Result first = qp::solve(P, q, A, lower, upper);
  const qp::WarmStart start = first.warm();
  const qp::Result restarted = qp::solve(P, q + 0.01, A, lower, upper, {}, start);
  const qp::Result cold = qp::solve(P, q + 0.01, A, lower, upper);

  out.writeln("warm start : iterations =", restarted.iterations);
  out.writeln("cold start : iterations =", cold.iterations);
}
```

### Example 2

Both reach the same answer: a warm start changes the work, not the result.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <limits>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace qp = pluss::subsp::math::qp;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(5));
  // minimise x0^2 + x1^2 - 2 x0 - 5 x1 = 0.5 x'Px + q'x   subject to   x0 + x1 = 1,  0 <= x0 <= 0.7,  x1 >= 0
  const double inf = std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity();
  const Series P = pluss::series::make({{2.0, 0.0}, {0.0, 2.0}});
  const Series q = pluss::series::make({-2.0, -5.0});
  const Series A = pluss::series::make({{1.0, 1.0}, {1.0, 0.0}, {0.0, 1.0}});
  const Series lower = pluss::series::make({1.0, 0.0, 0.0});
  const Series upper = pluss::series::make({1.0, 0.7, inf});

  const qp::Result first = qp::solve(P, q, A, lower, upper);
  const qp::Result restarted = qp::solve(P, q + 0.01, A, lower, upper, {}, first.warm());
  const qp::Result cold = qp::solve(P, q + 0.01, A, lower, upper);
  out.writeln("warm x =", restarted.x.items<double>());
  out.writeln("cold x =", cold.x.items<double>());
}
```

### Example 3

Built by hand. A starting guess for `x` alone, with `z` and `y` taken from a previous run, is a legal warm start; the three members are plain fields.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <limits>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace qp = pluss::subsp::math::qp;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(6));
  // minimise x0^2 + x1^2 - 2 x0 - 5 x1 = 0.5 x'Px + q'x   subject to   x0 + x1 = 1,  0 <= x0 <= 0.7,  x1 >= 0
  const double inf = std::numeric_limits<double>::infinity();
  const Series P = pluss::series::make({{2.0, 0.0}, {0.0, 2.0}});
  const Series q = pluss::series::make({-2.0, -5.0});
  const Series A = pluss::series::make({{1.0, 1.0}, {1.0, 0.0}, {0.0, 1.0}});
  const Series lower = pluss::series::make({1.0, 0.0, 0.0});
  const Series upper = pluss::series::make({1.0, 0.7, inf});

  const qp::Result first = qp::solve(P, q, A, lower, upper);
  qp::WarmStart guess;
  guess.x = pluss::series::make({0.1, 0.9});
  guess.z = first.z;
  guess.y = first.y;
  out.writeln("iterations from the hand-made start =", qp::solve(P, q, A, lower, upper, {}, guess).iterations);
}
```

